module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.two_source_files_claiming_one_module_path_misdiagnosed

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { FoundAtAmbiguous, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data two_source_files_claiming_one_module_path_misdiagnosed: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "two_source_files_claiming_one_module_path_misdiagnosed" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: FoundAtAmbiguous },

  receipts: [
    "**two source files claiming one module path are not refused as that collision, and the native door reports a later unbound name instead** (INVALID STATE: either two files declare the same module path, or one walked root's layout maps two files onto one tree identity, and ingest continues. HARM: the operator sequences on `resolve_unbound_name_is_declared_elsewhere` at a real token in an innocent module -- DESIGN section 5 silent wrongness wearing a located diagnostic.",

    "TRIGGER INCIDENT, lively-bear-908: dag/std/algebra.dag (`module std.algebra`) and src/v2/std/algebra.dag (`module std.algebra`) were loaded under ONE copied source root. Those are DISTINCT declared module paths. What collided was PATH-DERIVED SOURCE-ROOT IDENTITY: v2.compiler.source_authority classified a file as V2Tree from the file's storage spelling. A copied tree `/tmp/copy/src/v2/std/algebra.dag` did not start with `src/`, so both files became DagTree. Widening that classifier to 'contains /src/' moved the same silent misclassification onto a dag path such as `dag/pkg/src/leaf.dag`. Resolve then named a token in std.change as declared elsewhere rather than naming the layout.",

    "THE SAME DECLARED PATH IS A SECOND ARM OF THE SAME CLASS. The census already refused a second file declaring one QualifiedName (`closure_census_duplicate_module`), but located only that second file's module node. The first file was unnamed. v1's wall at v1_compiler.cli_run module_path_collision_panic_message names BOTH paths. A refusal that names one of two files is still a misdiagnosis of the collision.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACT: this is not `two_modules_answer_to_one_logical_qualified_name` (a logical-name strip after ingest). It is ingest identity: declared module path, or a tree tag guessed from the file's path, answering for two files as one.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: a native emit or census over a small tree reports a resolve unbound (once with a `resolve_unbound_name_is_declared_elsewhere` advisory, which compilation no longer produces) while two walked files share a declared module path OR a copied layout is tagged by file spelling rather than by the discovering --source-root. The reviewer tell: the diagnostic's file is not one of the two that share the identity.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: OUTSIDE THE LADDER -- silent wrongness. The later diagnostic is typed and located, and it is the wrong subject.",

    "CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed. Ingest can refuse a second file that declares an already-ingested module path, with loci on both files, and can stamp SourceRootRef from the walk origin that discovered the file, refusing when that origin is not a dag or src/v2 tree. It cannot make two files unwritable.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the native CLI, over a two-file tree whose declared module paths collide, refuses `closure_census_duplicate_module` naming both files; a copied single-root argument refuses as unrecognized rather than guessing DagTree; a file under --source-root dag whose path contains /src/ is still DagTree. Sufficient that a copied closure cannot present as an unbound name in a third module. Not satisfied by classifying the file path.",

    "CONSUMPTION: this per-class record joins the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster.",

    "SEED-SIDE SPELLING, FOUND BY THE v1 MODULE-INDEX ADMISSION INVENTORY (lane K, wave 2 of the v1 compile-performance plan, 2026-10-19; CORRECTED 2026-10-20 after a side-chat BLOCK restated the walk arms from the code; inventory only -- fix belongs to lane P / P1.2). INVALID STATE, stated from the walk arms: in build_module_path_index_uncached (src/v1/stage0/src/cli_run/entry_resolve.rs) the only early returns on an already-claimed module path are the same-file check (equal path key or same_canonical_file) and the root_idx > 0 exemption, and the populations behave differently: two DISTINCT files in the PRIMARY root (root_idx == 0) both reach insert_module_path and die at its collision panic, loudly; a distinct file in a LATER root shadowing a name an earlier root holds is skipped in silence under the documented primary-precedence policy (root[0] owns overlapping module paths; later roots contribute only absent modules); and a collision WITHIN one later root, against a name no earlier root holds, is likewise silent -- the first file is inserted, the second trips the same-name branch with root_idx > 0 and returns before insert_module_path is handed it, so for that population the name answers with a winner chosen by walk order, no diagnostic, no count. HARM: the path index carries ONE policy and serves it to every consumer, while the content index carries two -- the primary-precedence content builder (try_build_module_index_primary_precedence) SKIPS EXISTING NAMES FIRST, its pool_fill_only arm continuing past any name already indexed, agreeing with the path index silence; the STRICT content builder (try_build_module_index) REFUSES each of those silent populations, naming both decliners. A name-to-path consumer therefore gets a one-winner answer chosen by root and walk order on exactly the population where the strict door refuses -- DESIGN section 5 silent wrongness -- and the panic wall in the primary root reads as coverage for a collision class the later roots never reach. DISTINGUISHING FACT: this is the same ingest-identity collision as every receipt above, arriving at seed index construction, and arriving POLICY-ASYMMETRIC rather than merely silent -- the doors disagreeing about one population is the tell, visible by asking what the name-to-path index does with a collision the strict content index refuses. RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder -- silent wrongness for the later-root populations; the refusal exists in the sibling walk and is bypassed by this builder's single policy, so nothing downstream keys on it. CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed -- the same-name branch holds both keys already, existing and rel, both projected by module_index_path_key, so the skip can become a located refusal or a counted named report at zero extra reading; it cannot make two files unwritable. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: every consumer of build_module_path_index or with_module_path_index declares which policy its question is -- strict, or pool-fill primary-precedence -- and a seed gunbc run whose later root holds two DISTINCT files declaring one module path no earlier root holds refuses naming both files under a strict caller, and counts and names the skipped declaration under a pool-fill caller, where today both cases complete silent and exit 0 serving the walk-order winner; sufficient that no strict-policy name-to-path consumer answers one-winner where the strict content index of the same roots refuses, and the documented primary-precedence exemption stays honored, tested and visible rather than emergent.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.cli.repository_layout", decl_name: "repository_tree_of_root_argument", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.source_root_read", decl_name: "source_root_read", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.compiler.self_host.closure_emission", decl_name: "closure_duplicate_module_diagnostics", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
